WEBVTT

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:04.333
Different storage models make different questions
inexpensive.

00:00:04.333 --> 00:00:11.043
We will compare text retrieval with time-ordered
sensor data and make the lookup steps explicit.

00:00:11.043 --> 00:00:17.613
The useful choice follows the operation, including
how derived structures are kept up to date.

00:00:17.613 --> 00:00:21.107
A museum stores exhibit descriptions as documents.

00:00:21.107 --> 00:00:28.026
For keyword search, an inverted index might map
“bronze” to documents {4, 9, 12} and “river” to {9,

00:00:28.026 --> 00:00:28.306
15}.

00:00:28.306 --> 00:00:32.816
Their intersection returns document 9 for a query
requiring both words.

00:00:32.816 --> 00:00:38.597
This explains retrieval mechanics, but ranking and
language handling need additional rules.

00:00:38.597 --> 00:00:43.361
Updating the document also requires updating its
searchable representation.

00:00:43.361 --> 00:00:49.205
For temperature readings, a different design uses
sensor_id and day as the partition key and

00:00:49.205 --> 00:00:52.636
observation_time as the within-partition ordering
key.

00:00:52.636 --> 00:00:57.522
This supports one sensor’s daily range query while
bounding partition growth.

00:00:57.522 --> 00:01:01.901
Searching every sensor for one interval may need
another access path.

00:01:01.901 --> 00:01:06.724
A search index can lag its source, and a telemetry
partition can become hot.

00:01:06.724 --> 00:01:13.070
Neither a document shape nor a key-value interface
removes these workload and consistency questions.

00:01:13.070 --> 00:01:16.988
A cache is part of a data flow, not merely a faster
machine.

00:01:16.988 --> 00:01:22.147
Explain where its values come from, how they become
stale and how they recover.

00:01:22.147 --> 00:01:28.481
Our recreation-centre example separates a helpful
display from an authoritative booking decision.

00:01:28.481 --> 00:01:33.574
A recreation centre shows a cached count of
available places on its home page.

00:01:33.574 --> 00:01:39.346
The count can be briefly stale, but the booking
operation checks the authoritative reservation

00:01:39.346 --> 00:01:39.714
store.

00:01:39.714 --> 00:01:45.118
This separates a fast informational view from the
decision that commits scarce capacity.

00:01:45.118 --> 00:01:50.828
A time-to-live limits how long one cached value
remains eligible; it does not make that value

00:01:50.828 --> 00:01:52.363
current at every instant.

00:01:52.374 --> 00:01:58.655
For a weekly activity ranking, a sorted-set
abstraction can update one participant’s score
without

00:01:58.655 --> 00:02:00.514
rewriting the entire ranking.

00:02:00.514 --> 00:02:06.795
Before adopting it, decide whether the ranking can
be rebuilt from durable events and what happens

00:02:06.795 --> 00:02:08.461
after eviction or restart.

00:02:08.461 --> 00:02:12.947
Also plan how simultaneous cache misses avoid
overwhelming the origin.

00:02:12.974 --> 00:02:18.801
A reverse-proxy cache of a whole public page sits at
a different layer from these application-

00:02:18.801 --> 00:02:22.025
managed values, even when both reduce repeated work.

00:02:22.074 --> 00:02:26.645
Files, objects and blocks expose different
operations to applications.

00:02:26.645 --> 00:02:31.673
Choose the interface by following a real workflow,
including partial failure.

00:02:31.673 --> 00:02:37.812
A wildlife recording archive helps us connect
storage selection with the responsibilities that

00:02:37.812 --> 00:02:40.423
appear between two independent services.

00:02:40.423 --> 00:02:46.953
A wildlife archive stores short audio recordings as
objects and keeps their searchable metadata in a

00:02:46.953 --> 00:02:47.541
database.

00:02:47.574 --> 00:02:53.802
Clients request a recording by an object identifier
rather than mounting the archive as a disk.

00:02:53.802 --> 00:03:00.358
A legacy analysis program that expects directories
and file operations may instead need a filesystem

00:03:00.358 --> 00:03:01.013
interface.

00:03:01.013 --> 00:03:07.569
A block volume provides lower-level storage on which
a filesystem can be created; it is not itself a

00:03:07.569 --> 00:03:08.880
shared file service.

00:03:08.880 --> 00:03:12.907
The recording and its metadata form a cross-system
workflow.

00:03:12.907 --> 00:03:19.351
If an upload succeeds but the metadata write fails,
the archive needs a cleanup or retry rule so

00:03:19.351 --> 00:03:21.767
abandoned objects do not accumulate.

00:03:21.767 --> 00:03:27.271
If metadata is published before upload completion,
users may receive broken links.

00:03:27.271 --> 00:03:30.358
Draw these failure paths before adding stores.

00:03:30.358 --> 00:03:36.735
The final architecture should identify ownership,
identifiers, access control, recovery and the

00:03:36.735 --> 00:03:39.822
reason each separate abstraction is necessary.
